  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\n08Z water vapor imagery placed an upper low over southern Canada \nwith the trough axis extending south into UT and AZ. Surface obs \nshowed a prefrontal trough axis extended from northeast NEB into \nsouthwest CO with a cold front into central NEB and northwest KS. \n\nThe latest model guidance continues to bring the cold front through \nthe forecast area this afternoon and evening with the better \ndynamics progged to lift across the front this evening. All of the \nsolutions show a similar idea with showers and storms along the \nboundary. So there isn't a lot of change to the prev forecast for \ntoday or tonight. As for severe weather potential, the RAP and RRFS \nshow very modest MUCAPE of a couple hundred J/kg with mid level \nlapse rates around 7 C/km. These lapse rates and available \ninstability are not expected to last long as once precip falls \nthrough the column, wetbulb cooling causes lapse rates to trend \ntowards the moist adiabat. With the limited instability expected, I \ndon't have any issue with the general thunderstorm forecast in the \nlatest SWODY1. There is expected to be plenty of moisture for \nthunderstorms to work with. Precipital water values are forecast to \nbe up to 1.25 inches which is close to the maximum value for this \nday. So the storms are likely to produce heavy rain. Fortunately \nthey should remain fairly progressive with storm motion generally to \nthe northeast at 15 to 20KT. Additionally one hour flash flood \nguidance is around 2 inches. So flooding concerns appear to be \nlimited to nuisance type ponding of water. \n\nThe cold front should be through the area by Sunday morning with \nmuch dryer air clearing things out. Another upper level disturbance \nis progged to come out of the Rockies Monday night and Tuesday. But \nvery dry air within the Canadian surface ridge is expected to \npreclude any precip chances. The forecast predictability decreases \nfor Wednesday through Friday as models struggle with upper level \nenergy closing off just down stream of the forecast area. Based on \ncluster analysis of the ensembles, the main uncertainty for the \nforecast area is how quickly temps may moderate. So overall the \nforecast is for dry weather through the end of the week. Below \nnormal temps to begin the week should trend towards seasonal normals \nby the end of the week. At this time there are no apparent regional \ntravel concerns leading up to or after Thanksgiving.",
